// @check
// @error `+` over Circle[] has no lowering
// @error `addT` concatenates its type parameter with `+` here: `+` over Circle[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over f64[] has no lowering
// @error `addT` concatenates its type parameter with `+` here: `+` over f64[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over string[] has no lowering
// @error `addT` concatenates its type parameter with `+` here: `+` over string[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over {[string]: i32}[] has no lowering
// @error `addT` concatenates its type parameter with `+` here: `+` over {[string]: i32}[] has no lowering
// @error operator '+' is not defined for K and K (the call's argument types)
// @error operator '+' is not defined for string and string
// @error `+` over F[] has no lowering
// @error `addT` concatenates its type parameter with `+` here: `+` over F[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over G[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over BIG[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over I[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over M[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over (N | null)[] has no lowering
// @error `+` over (F | null)[] has no lowering
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D44 — D35's shape one operator over, and TWO ROOTS
// rather than one. The ablation that separates them is the ELEMENT type: a literal union goes
// through the first, a list element through the second, and neither fix reaches the other's
// cells.
//
// ROOT ONE — THE PIN SOFTENED BEFORE ITS STRING ARM AND `checkBinary` SOFTENS AFTER.
// `binOpDefinedFor` ran `softenLitTy` on both operands and THEN asked `isStringTy`, so at
// `T = "a" | "b"` it saw two `string`s and admitted a concat. The direct spelling reaches its
// own softening only after string concat and list concat have both declined, and reports
// `operator '+' is not defined for string and string`. One decision, two severities, chosen by
// the spelling — and the pin's was the permissive one, over a module the engine refuses. The
// arm now mirrors `checkBinary`'s order exactly; the two last lines above are the pair.
//
// ROOT TWO — "ANY TWO ARRAYS ARE A LIST CONCAT" WAS A RULE NOBODY HELD. There is exactly ONE
// concat core (`emitListConcatI`, the i32 backing). `listOpKindOfBin` names three list kinds
// and the emitter refuses two of them outright; every element rep it does not claim at all —
// an f64/f32/i64/u8 list, a struct list, a map list, a closure list, a boxed-nullable list —
// fell PAST the list arm into the NUMERIC tail and emitted `i32.add` over two refs. So this
// half is not a refusal the pin dropped: it is a refusal that existed at neither spelling for
// the reps that mattered most, stated at the pin as though it did.
//
// THE ACCEPT SET IS THE i32 BACKING AND IT WAS MEASURED, NOT DERIVED. Every element rep was
// RUN at both spellings first, and the seven that run — `i32`, `boolean`, a string literal
// union, a numeric literal union with an `i32` base, a `new i32` newtype, `boolean | null` and
// `K | null` (both i32 SENTINELS, unlike the `i32 | null` BOX beside them) — still run. Their
// values are pinned in `tests/cases/literal-unions/litunion-list-concat-backing.vl`, which is
// the accept set written down so a later narrowing that over-refuses goes red rather than
// quietly removing a capability.
//
// **THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO OPEN "`concatElemIsI32Backed` is NOT `eqCmpKindOfArrayElem`: that
// one answers "none" for a literal-union element", AND BOTH HALVES OF THAT ARE GONE.** The
// `==` home grew the literal family in D45 and its two sentinel-nullable siblings in D101, and
// this home turned out to be WIDER than its own sentence claimed in the direction that is a
// defect rather than a capability. `tyUnionAllLits` accepted EVERY literal base and the
// `TyLit` arm accepted every single literal, so THREE spellings whose rep is not an i32 cell
// were claimed as the i32 list and wrote modules the engine refuses at `vl check` rc 0 —
// `type F = 1.5 | 2.5` (the filed row), `type G = 1.5` and `type BIG = 9999999999`, the last
// two at a different offset and sentence from the first, which is why the row's own one-line
// fix (the union arm alone) would have closed one third of it. `silent-class-inventory` D102.
//
// THE TWO HOMES NOW ASK ONE PREDICATE for this family — `tyLitUnionIsI32Backed` and
// `tyIsI32SentinelNullable` — so `+` and `==` can no longer answer differently about the same
// cell, which is the drift both headers exist to prevent and which each of them reproduced
// once. `concatElemIsI32Backed` still DEFAULTS TO ACCEPT for a rep it has not measured (a
// hole, a type variable); what changed is that the literal family is now measured rather than
// waved through.
//
// THE FLOOR MOVED WITH IT. `binConcatHasNoLowering` is the `+` twin of `binEqHasNoLowering`,
// reading the same one home, so a spelling that reaches emit without passing either gate gets
// a loud `emitProgram:` failure instead of the numeric tail's `i32.add` over two refs.
type Circle = { r: i32 }
type CM = {[string]: i32}
type K = "a" | "b"
type N = 1 | 2
type F = 1.5 | 2.5
type I = 9999999999 | 1
type M = "a" | 1
type G = 1.5
type BIG = 9999999999

function addT<T>(a: T, b: T): T { a + b }
function mkm(): CM { const m: CM = Map(); m["k"] = 1; return m }

// ROOT TWO, at four element reps and BOTH spellings. `Circle[]` and `CM[]` were the row's own
// two cells (loud emit written directly, check-clean invalid wasm through the pin); `f64[]` and
// `string[]` are the halves the row disclaimed as a `+`-lowering question, and writing the
// boundary once answers all four.
function structListDirect(a: Circle[], b: Circle[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function structListPinned(a: Circle[], b: Circle[]): i32 { addT(a, b).length }

function f64ListDirect(a: f64[], b: f64[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function f64ListPinned(a: f64[], b: f64[]): i32 { addT(a, b).length }

function stringListDirect(a: string[], b: string[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function stringListPinned(a: string[], b: string[]): i32 { addT(a, b).length }

function mapListDirect(a: CM[], b: CM[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function mapListPinned(a: CM[], b: CM[]): i32 { addT(a, b).length }

// ROOT ONE — the literal union, where the PIN was the permissive spelling. The two messages
// differ on purpose and the difference is a diagnostic-quality note the row records: the direct
// arm has already softened `K` to `string` by the time it reports, the pin has not.
function litUnionPinned(a: K, b: K): string { addT(a, b) }
function litUnionDirect(a: K, b: K): string { a + b }

print(structListDirect([{ r: 1 }], [{ r: 2 }]))
print(structListPinned([{ r: 1 }], [{ r: 2 }]))
print(f64ListDirect([1.5], [2.5]))
print(f64ListPinned([1.5], [2.5]))
print(stringListDirect(["p"], ["q"]))
print(stringListPinned(["p"], ["q"]))
print(mapListDirect([mkm()], [mkm()]))
print(mapListPinned([mkm()], [mkm()]))
// ── D102: THE LITERAL FAMILY, SPLIT BY BACKING. The three that were check-clean INVALID WASM
//    come first — an f64-based literal UNION, and the two SINGLE literals whose base is not an
//    i32 either. `f64[] + f64[]` and `i64[] + i64[]` are refused written directly (the rows
//    above), so these now say what their own base type says, which is the whole rule. ──
function f64UnionDirect(a: F[], b: F[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function f64UnionPinned(a: F[], b: F[]): i32 { addT(a, b).length }
function f64LitDirect(a: G[], b: G[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function i64LitDirect(a: BIG[], b: BIG[]): i32 { (a + b).length }

// ── the two literal unions with no ONE base at all — an i64-based one and a MIXED-base one
//    (`"a" | 1` is every-member-a-literal and reps as a string ref beside an i32, so it is a
//    value-union BOX and not one cell). Both were a loud `emitProgram:` before, which is the
//    same answer arriving later and with a sentence about a struct field. ──
function i64UnionDirect(a: I[], b: I[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function mixedUnionDirect(a: M[], b: M[]): i32 { (a + b).length }

// ── the NUMERIC litunion's nullable, which is a value-union BOX where the STRING one is the
//    i32 atom sentinel. `(K | null)[] + (K | null)[]` runs and is pinned in the backing
//    fixture; these two are its boundary, and the spelling is identical. ──
function nulNumUnionDirect(a: (N | null)[], b: (N | null)[]): i32 { (a + b).length }
function nulF64UnionDirect(a: (F | null)[], b: (F | null)[]): i32 { (a + b).length }

print(litUnionPinned("a", "b"))
print(litUnionDirect("a", "b"))
print(f64UnionDirect([1.5], [2.5]))
print(f64UnionPinned([1.5], [2.5]))
print(f64LitDirect([1.5], [1.5]))
print(i64LitDirect([9999999999], [9999999999]))
print(i64UnionDirect([1], [1]))
print(mixedUnionDirect(["a"], [1]))
print(nulNumUnionDirect([1], [null]))
print(nulF64UnionDirect([1.5], [null]))
